1. Terraform Architecture
    - Terraform follows a simple architecture where the Terraform CLI interacts with providers, APIs, and a state file to manage infrastructure.
        +-------------------+
        | Terraform Config  |
        | (.tf files)       |
        +---------+---------+
                |
                v
        +-------------------+
        | Terraform CLI     |
        | init/plan/apply   |
        +---------+---------+
                |
                v
        +-------------------+
        | Provider Plugins  |
        | AWS/Azure/GCP/K8s |
        +---------+---------+
                |
                v
        +-------------------+
        | Cloud APIs        |
        | Resources Created |
        +-------------------+

                ^
                |
        +-------------------+
        | Terraform State   |
        | terraform.tfstate |
        +-------------------+

2. Components:
    - 1. Configuration Files
        These are .tf files written in HashiCorp Configuration Language (HCL).
        Example:
            resource "aws_instance" "web" {
                ami           = "ami-123456"
                instance_type = "t2.micro"
            }
        They define the desired infrastructure.
    - 2. Terraform Core
        Terraform Core is the engine that:
            Reads .tf files
            Builds a dependency graph
            Compares desired state with current state
            Creates an execution plan
            Coordinates provider plugins
        Commands:
            terraform init
            terraform plan
            terraform apply
            terraform destroy
    - 3. Providers
        Providers act as translators between Terraform and external platforms.
        Examples:
            Amazon Web Services Provider
            Microsoft Azure Provider
            Google Cloud Platform Provider
            Kubernetes Provider
            GitHub Provider
        Example:
            provider "aws" {
                region = "ap-south-1"
            }
        When Terraform needs to create an EC2 instance, it sends requests through the AWS provider, which calls AWS APIs.
    - 4. State File
        Terraform stores infrastructure information in a state file:
            terraform.tfstate
        The state file contains:
            Resource IDs
            Metadata
            Resource relationships
            Current infrastructure status
        Terraform uses this file to determine what changes are needed.
    - 5. Resource Graph
        Terraform automatically builds a dependency graph.
        Example:
            VPC
            |
            Subnet
            |
            EC2 Instance
        Terraform creates resources in the correct order and can execute independent resources in parallel.

    - 6. Execution Plan
        When you run:
            terraform plan
        Terraform compares:
            Desired State (.tf files)
                    VS
            Current State (tfstate)
        It then shows:
            Resources to create
            Resources to modify
            Resources to destroy
        Nothing is changed during plan.
    - 7. Apply Phase
        When you run:
            terraform apply
        Terraform:
            Reads configuration
            Reads state
            Calls provider APIs
            Creates/updates resources
            Updates state file